## Value Added Models (VAMs): 
```{r}
set.seed(141229)
n <- 80

students <- tibble(
  prior_score = rnorm(n, 50, 12),
  noise       = rnorm(n, 0, 6)
) |>
  mutate(
    current_score = 20 + 0.65 * prior_score + noise
  )

# Fit regression
fit <- lm(current_score ~ prior_score, data = students)
students$predicted <- predict(fit)

# One highlighted student
student <- tibble(prior_score = 55, predicted = predict(fit, newdata = tibble(prior_score = 55)))
student$current_score <- student$predicted + 12  # above the line = positive teacher effect

ggplot(students, aes(x = prior_score, y = current_score)) +
  geom_point(alpha = 0.25, color = "grey60", size = 2) +
  geom_smooth(method = "lm", se = TRUE, color = "grey30",
              linewidth = 1, linetype = "dashed", fill = "grey90") +
  # Highlighted student point
  geom_point(data = student, aes(x = prior_score, y = current_score),
             color = "#1D9E75", size = 5) +
  # Dot on the regression line
  geom_point(data = student, aes(x = prior_score, y = predicted),
             color = "grey30", size = 4, shape = 4, stroke = 1.5) +
  # Arrow showing teacher effect
  geom_segment(data = student,
               aes(x = prior_score, xend = prior_score,
                   y = predicted, yend = current_score),
               color = "#D85A30", linewidth = 1.4,
               arrow = arrow(ends = "both", length = unit(0.1, "inches"))) +
  # Dashed horizontal line from prior score
  geom_segment(data = student,
               aes(x = prior_score, xend = prior_score,
                   y = min(students$current_score), 
                   yend = predicted),
               color = "grey50", linetype = "dotted", linewidth = 0.8) +
  geom_segment(data = student,
               aes(x = min(students$prior_score), 
                   xend = prior_score,
                   y = predicted, yend = predicted),
               color = "grey50", linetype = "dotted", linewidth = 0.8) +
  # Labels
  annotate("text",
           x = student$prior_score + 2,
           y = (student$predicted + student$current_score) / 2,
           label = "Teacher Effect\n(+12 points)",
           color = "#D85A30", size = 4, fontface = "bold", hjust = 0) +
  annotate("text",
           x = student$prior_score + 2,
           y = student$current_score + 1.5,
           label = "Actual score",
           color = "#1D9E75", size = 3.5, hjust = 0) +
  annotate("text",
           x = student$prior_score - 18,
           y = student$predicted + 1.5,
           label = "Expected score",
           color = "grey30", size = 3.5, hjust = 0, fontface = "italic") +
  annotate("text",
           x = 28, 
           y = predict(fit, newdata = tibble(prior_score = 28)) - 4,
           label = "Population average\n(prior achievement only)",
           color = "grey30", size = 3, hjust = 0, fontface = "italic") +
  labs(
    x = "Prior year score",
    y = "Current year score"
  ) +
  theme_minimal(base_size = 13) +
  theme(panel.grid.minor = element_blank())
```


## The Rise of Teacher Effects:

Initially grew traction as an alternative to No Child Left Behind act's strict proficiency thresholds, aimed at increasing education quality through standardized testing and improved teacher quality. 

![](figs/rttt.jpg){fig-align="center" width="50%"}

Race to the Top (RttT) 2009: Increased emphasis and resources for the rigorous interventions needed to turn around the lowest-performing schools. The unintended consequence of this program was the widespread use and development of value added models. 

# Objective: 
::: {style="text-align: center;"}
### Create a low variability, low bias, single step VAM, using combinations of existing SWVAMs and generalized linear mixed models.

:::


## Contextualized Value-Added Model (CVA):

The Contextualized Value-Added model improves upon the baseline VA model (a simple regression) with the inclusion of student-level demographic covariates, thereby reducing bias attributable to the non-random assignment of students to teachers. The model is specified as:

$$
\begin{equation}
    y_{it} = \mu_0 + \beta \, y_{i,t-1} + \boldsymbol{\gamma}^{\top} \mathbf{X}_{it}
             + \theta_{j(i)} + \varepsilon_{it}
\end{equation}
$$


---

## Signal-Weighted Value-Added Model (SWVAM): 

Kim (2022) proposes SWVAM to address the high variance in teacher effect estimates by introducing student-level signal parameters. It includes two additional latent parameters for each student: 

![](figs/pete.jpg){fig-align="center" width="40%"}

> $a_i \geq 0$ **sensitivity**: the degree to which student $i$'s
          achievement responds to their assigned teacher's quality; a student with
          high $a_i$ contributes more information about teacher effectiveness

> $r_i \in [0, 1]$ **reliability**: the consistency with which
          student $i$'s scores reflect teacher quality across time; a student with
          high $r_i$ produces stable signals across repeated observations





---

## Composite Model


**Applies both variance reduction and bias reduction in one step, thus allowing for signal weighting and covariate adjustment to inform each other, avoiding error propagation in a two step model. **



>**SWVAM**
$$y_{ijt} = \mu_t + a_i\theta_j + r_i\eta_i + \varepsilon_{ijt}$$
Applied to residuals


>**Composite**
$$y_{ijt} = \mu_t + a_i\theta_j + r_i\eta_i + \underbrace{\mu_0 + \beta y_{i,t-1} + \gamma^\top X_{it} + \delta P_{it} + \zeta^\top S_j}_{\text{bias reduction}} + \varepsilon_{ijt}$$
Applied to data



---


## Tennessee Value-Added Assessment System (TVAAS):

Using each student's complete testing history, this model assumes that socioeconomic and background influence persist across prior score trajectory. 



